  <section id="formats" aria-labelledby="formats-h">
    <h2 id="formats-h">How to Write Height: The Main Formats</h2>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table"><caption>Common ways to write the same height</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Format</th><th scope="col">Example</th><th scope="col">Use it for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">ft and in</th><td>5 ft 10 in</td><td>Everyday writing, forms</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Prime symbols</th><td>5&prime;10&Prime;</td><td>Charts, profiles, design</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Apostrophe and quote</th><td>5'10"</td><td>Plain text, email</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Spelled out</th><td>5 feet 10 inches</td><td>Formal writing, running text</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Hyphenated adjective</th><td>a 5-foot-10 man</td><td>Before a noun (AP style)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Sports shorthand</th><td>5-10</td><td>Rosters, sports news</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Centimeters</th><td>178 cm</td><td>Metric countries, medicine</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Meters</th><td>1.78 m</td><td>Passports, science</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  </section>

  <section id="symbols" aria-labelledby="symbols-h">
    <h2 id="symbols-h">Feet and Inches Symbols</h2>
    <p>The symbol for feet is the <strong>prime</strong> (&prime;) and the symbol for inches is the <strong>double prime</strong> (&Prime;). On most keyboards they are replaced with the straight apostrophe (') and straight quotation mark ("), which everyone understands. Put no space between the number and the symbol: 5&prime;10&Prime;, not 5 &prime; 10 &Prime;.</p>
    <p>Avoid curly (smart) quotes such as 5&rsquo;10&rdquo;: they are punctuation, not measurement marks, and can break spreadsheets and search.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="style" aria-labelledby="style-h">
    <h2 id="style-h">Height in AP, Chicago and Medical Style</h2>
    <ul class="tp-steps" style="list-style:disc">
      <li><strong>AP style (news):</strong> use figures and spell out the units: &ldquo;He is 5 feet 10 inches tall.&rdquo; Before a noun, hyphenate: &ldquo;the 5-foot-10 forward&rdquo;. Sports copy may use 5-10.</li>
      <li><strong>Chicago style:</strong> spell out whole numbers under 100 in non-technical text (&ldquo;five feet ten inches&rdquo;) but use figures with abbreviations or in technical work.</li>
      <li><strong>Medical and scientific:</strong> use metric units, usually centimeters to one decimal (177.8 cm) or meters (1.78 m).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="metric" aria-labelledby="metric-h">
    <h2 id="metric-h">Writing Height in Centimeters and Meters</h2>
    <p>Most of the world writes height in centimeters (178 cm) or meters (1.78 m). Leave a space between the number and the unit, use no period after cm or m, and never add an s. To convert quickly, use <a href="/cm-to-feet-and-inches/">cm to feet and inches</a>, <a href="/feet-to-cm/">feet to cm</a> or <a href="/inches-to-cm/">inches to cm</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="mistakes" aria-labelledby="mistakes-h">
    <h2 id="mistakes-h">Common Mistakes When Writing Height</h2>
    <ul class="tp-steps" style="list-style:disc">
      <li><strong>5.10 ft for 5&prime;10&Prime;:</strong> 5.10 feet means 5 feet 1.2 inches. Use 5.83 ft if you need decimal feet.</li>
      <li><strong>Mixing units:</strong> avoid &ldquo;5 feet 178&rdquo;; give one system, or both with the second in brackets.</li>
      <li><strong>&ldquo;cms&rdquo; or &ldquo;cm.&rdquo;:</strong> the symbol is always cm.</li>
      <li><strong>Missing the zero:</strong> write 6&prime;0&Prime; or 6 ft, not 6&prime;&Prime;.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Want to see what a height looks like? Try the <a href="/height-comparison/">height comparison tool</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-h">
    <h2 id="faq-h">Writing Height FAQ</h2>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>How do you write height in feet and inches?</h3></summary><p>Write the feet, then the inches: 5 ft 10 in, or with symbols 5′10″. The single mark (′) means feet and the double mark (″) means inches. In running text, spell it out: 5 feet 10 inches.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What is the correct way to write 5 foot 10?</h3></summary><p>In everyday writing, “5 ft 10 in” or “5′10″”. Before a noun, news style (AP) uses hyphens: “a 5-foot-10 guard”. After a verb, use “feet”: “she is 5 feet 10 inches tall” or “5-10” in sports writing.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Do you write 5’10” or 5′10″?</h3></summary><p>Both are understood. The prime symbols (′ and ″) are typographically correct; straight apostrophes and quotation marks (5'10") are fine in plain text, emails and forms. Avoid curly quotes (’ and ”), which some systems misread.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Is it 5 feet or 5 foot?</h3></summary><p>Use “feet” after a number when it is a measurement (5 feet tall) and “foot” in a hyphenated adjective before a noun (a 5-foot-tall fence, a 6-foot man). “5 foot 10” is common in speech and informal writing.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>How do you write height in centimeters?</h3></summary><p>Write the number, a space and cm: 178 cm. Do not add a period or an s (not cm. or cms). In meters, use two decimals: 1.78 m.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>How do you write height on a form or resume?</h3></summary><p>Use the format the form asks for. Medical and government forms often want centimeters or total inches (70 in). Dating and casting profiles usually use 5′10″ or 178 cm; adding both is helpful for international readers.</p></details>
  </section>

